From tplate at blackmesacapital.com  Tue Aug 27 17:55:16 2002
From: tplate at blackmesacapital.com (Tony Plate)
Date: Tue, 27 Aug 2002 11:55:16 -0600
Subject: Re: subtleties of classes,methods??
In-Reply-To: <777F3F91D3F3D4118CCA00508B953E1904404884@usrymx06.merck.com>
References: <777F3F91D3F3D4118CCA00508B953E1904404884@usrymx06.merck.com>
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.2.20020827114807.047f8180@mailhost.blackmesacapital.com>

The second argument to do.call() is a *list of arguments* to the function, 
and a data frame is a list.  So, when you do do.call('boxplot', dataframe) 
or do.call("boxplot", as.list(dataframe)), boxplot gets called with 
multiple arguments: the columns of the data frame.  But, when you do 
do.call("boxplot", list(dataframe)), boxplot gets just one argument: the 
dataframe.

Easily verified:

> foo <- function(...) browser()
> do.call("foo", data.frame(a=1:3,b=4:6))
b(foo)> list(...)
$a:
[1] 1 2 3

$b:
[1] 4 5 6

b(foo)> c
NULL
> do.call("foo", as.list(data.frame(a=1:3,b=4:6)))
Called from: foo(a = c(1, 2, 3), b = c(....
b(foo)> list(...)
$a:
[1] 1 2 3

$b:
[1] 4 5 6

b(foo)> c
NULL
> do.call("foo", list(data.frame(a=1:3,b=4:6)))
Called from: foo(structure(.Data = list....
b(foo)> list(...)
[[1]]:
  a b
1 1 4
2 2 5
3 3 6

-- Tony Plate

